A||N||7||DaveL||Ultimate Dogbone !||||10||
Z||000000||DaveL||12-06-2000||09:21 AM||||Well, my 87 Se 2.5 had started making a nasty clunk when putting the auto into gear. I replaced the dogbone with an oem equivalent. That didn't change anything. A trip to the mechanic resulted in a new motor mount. But still it clunked. So I bought one of those ultimate dogbones from the Fiero Store, and it made an amazing difference! My guess is that driving it around for a week with a broken motor mount ruined the new dogbone. Scary when you think cvj's are going! I'll never use the oem strut again, although I wonder what happens when one of the ultimate dogbones decides to retire?<BR>The oem engine strut setup is for the birds! It also seems to have resolved a delayed shift problem I've been having. How can that be?||||reg||10
Z||000001||1FST2M6||12-06-2000||10:34 AM||||if you still have the clunking check the exhaust hangers.. the exhaust on both my cars (SE, GT)barely hangs on .. the muffler shop didn't do such a good job..  we had to weld 2 new support tabs to the frame at the back of the oil pan and right at the bend on the passengerside to push the down so it wouldn't bang, and scrape going over speed bumps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||12-06-2000||10:39 AM||||Maybe I wasn't clear, the dogbone completely fixed the problem! Guzzinta any gear, no clunk whatsoever! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-06-2000||10:48 AM||||Even in brand new stock form all the mounts still flexed when shifting. The poly moves a lot less so the torque isn't lost. The poly dog bone will last for almost but not quite, forever. You probably noticed that you can feel the engine vibration a little more now too. If you really want to get a great feeling on take off's, replace the cradle bushings with poly also. It doesn't affect your 0-60 times but it will launch off the line very hard when there is no slop in the mounts. Even with a Duke it'll push you in the seat on take-off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaveL||12-06-2000||10:51 AM||||How difficult are the cradle bushings to replace?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||12-06-2000||12:15 PM||||The best way to do it is coat the bolts with WD-40 or other penetrating oil for about a week then you can remove bolts and lower cradle. The rear set is easy to swap. The front set (where cradle is hinged) is a pain because you need to burn out old rubber bushings and press in the new poly. Pressing in is easy, Burning out old is smelly/messy.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-06-2000||07:18 PM||||Let me guess.... 87 2.5l with automatic transmission. read the article in my cave about the torque struts. It tells why your new OE strut didn't work.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaveL||12-07-2000||08:19 AM||||Ogre's cave is where I got the idea to replace the original strut with the UDB. There is a wealth of material there. Enough for even the most dedicated, devoted, possessed, slightly insane, hell bent Fiero fanatics around!<P>I got rid of the worst noise in my Fiero when I got rid of my wife....||||reg||10
